# Replace every per-country prepopulated engine list with one fixed
# privacy-focused set: Startpage (default), DuckDuckGo, Brave Search, Mojeek,
# Qwant, Ecosia, degoog and the two DuckDuckGo no-JS variants. Brave, Mojeek,
# Qwant and Ecosia are already defined upstream (ids 109, 103, 94, 101), so
# they cost one list entry each and no new definition. ungoogled-chromium replaces the Google engine
# with a "No Search" stub, so without this the omnibox cannot search at all
# until the user configures an engine by hand.
#
# ID allocation: the three new engines take the next free IDs after upstream's
# highest (116), and kMaxPrepopulatedEngineID is raised to match, exactly as
# prepopulated_engines.json's own comment instructs. IDs must stay <= 1000:
#   - components/search_engines/template_url_data.cc GenerateGUID() only emits
#     the deterministic sync GUID for prepopulate_id in [1, 1000]. Anything
#     above gets a fresh random UUID per construction, which is what the
#     deterministic GUID exists to prevent ("to make sure sync doesn't incur in
#     duplicates for prepopulated engines") - two synced devices would each
#     mint their own row for the same engine, leaving duplicate keywords in the
#     keyword database.
#   - search_engine_choice_service.cc treats prepopulate_id >
#     kMaxPrepopulatedEngineID as "distribution custom search engine". Pushing
#     kMaxPrepopulatedEngineID to 1003 to cover 1001+ IDs silently disabled
#     that classification for every real distribution-custom engine too.
# On a Chromium version bump, check whether upstream has claimed 117-119 for
# its own engines before re-applying - see UPDATING.md.

# Rebased onto Chromium 152: upstream moved kCurrentDataVersion 209 -> 210 for
# its own data changes, so this file's value moves with it. It has to keep
# increasing for existing Aerium profiles to pick the new list up at all, and
# the last release shipped 251. kMaxPrepopulatedEngineID is still 116 upstream,
# so ids 117-119 remain free and the guard below has not fired.
#
# 253 now, because degoog replaced SearXNG. Raise this on EVERY change to the
# engine list, not only when an id is added: the re-merge into an existing
# profile is gated on a strict inequality - ComputeDatabaseUpdateRequirements
# in template_url_prepopulate_data_resolver.cc returns nullopt unless the
# database's builtin_keyword_data_version is strictly BELOW this - so a value
# that does not move reaches nobody who already has a profile, and the entry
# is invisible outside a fresh install.
#
# degoog reuses SearXNG's id 119 rather than taking 120. Chromium keys a
# prepopulated engine's row by prepopulate_id, so reusing it rewrites the
# SearXNG row shipped in earlier builds into the degoog row on update instead
# of leaving an orphan beside a new entry. That also keeps the id range at
# 117-119 and leaves kMaxPrepopulatedEngineID where it is.
#
# The three URLs are the ones degoog publishes in its own OpenSearch document
# at https://degoog.org/opensearch.xml, not inferred from the address bar, and
# all three were fetched: /search?q= answers 200, the suggest endpoint returns
# well-formed OpenSearch JSON, and the favicon is a real image/x-icon.

--- a/components/regional_capabilities/regional_capabilities_utils.cc
+++ b/components/regional_capabilities/regional_capabilities_utils.cc
@@ -46,14 +46,20 @@ GetPrepopulatedEnginesTestOverrideInternal() {
 // category.
 constexpr size_t kTopSearchEnginesThreshold = 5;
 
-// Returns regional settings appropriate for the provided country. If no
-// specific regional settings are defined, returns the default settings.
-const RegionalSettings& GetRegionalSettings(CountryId country_id) {
-  auto iter = TemplateURLPrepopulateData::kRegionalSettings.find(country_id);
-  if (iter == TemplateURLPrepopulateData::kRegionalSettings.end()) {
-    // Fallback to default country.
-    iter = TemplateURLPrepopulateData::kRegionalSettings.find(CountryId());
-  }
+// Aerium: every country gets the same privacy-focused engine list - the "ZZ"
+// default in regional_settings.json - instead of per-country Google-led lists,
+// so the requested country is deliberately ignored.
+const RegionalSettings& GetRegionalSettings(
+    [[maybe_unused]] CountryId country_id) {
+  // A default-constructed CountryId is the "ZZ" unknown-country key (see
+  // country_codes.h), which regional_settings.json always defines, so this
+  // lookup cannot miss. Upstream's "fall back to the default country" branch
+  // is dropped rather than kept: with the primary lookup already using
+  // CountryId() the fallback repeated the identical query, so it could never
+  // recover - it only left a path that dereferences end(). The CHECK states
+  // the data invariant instead of relying on undefined behaviour.
+  auto iter = TemplateURLPrepopulateData::kRegionalSettings.find(CountryId());
+  CHECK(iter != TemplateURLPrepopulateData::kRegionalSettings.end());
 
   return *iter->second;
 }
